    <fr:title text="Small-Field Commitments and Ring Switching">Small-Field Commitments and Ring Switching</fr:title>
  </fr:frontmatter>
  <fr:mainmatter>
    <html:p>
    Committing to values that live in a tiny field without paying extension-field prices for every one of them. The problem <fr:link href="/dhsorens-003J/" title="Binius" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-003J/" display-uri="dhsorens-003J" type="local">Binius</fr:link> exists to solve, and the reason ArkLib has a <html:code>RingSwitching</html:code> directory.
</html:p>
    <html:p>
    Questions this page should answer:
    <html:ul><html:li>Why a naive commitment to <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathbb {F}_2]]></fr:tex> values wastes almost all of its capacity, and what packing recovers.</html:li>
        <html:li>The ring-switching reduction: a claim over the extension becomes a claim over the base, or the reverse.</html:li>
        <html:li>Where soundness needs the large field regardless, and how much of the argument can stay small.</html:li>
        <html:li>The interaction with <fr:link href="/dhsorens-002L/" title="Binary Tower Fields" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-002L/" display-uri="dhsorens-002L" type="local">binary towers</fr:link> specifically, versus 31-bit primes.</html:li>
        <html:li>What ArkLib formalises here and what remains open.</html:li></html:ul></html:p>
    <html:p>
    Sources: <fr:link href="https://github.com/Verified-zkEVM/ArkLib" type="external">ArkLib</fr:link>, <fr:link href="https://eprint.iacr.org/2026/1371" type="external">The Small-Field Turn</fr:link>.
</html:p>
  </fr:mainmatter>
