            <fr:title text="Polynomial Commitment Schemes">Polynomial Commitment Schemes</fr:title>
            <fr:meta name="toc">true</fr:meta>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
            <html:p>
    The component that turns an oracle in an <fr:link href="/dhsorens-002E/" title="Interactive Proofs, Arguments, and IOPs" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-002E/" display-uri="dhsorens-002E" type="local">IOP</fr:link> into something a real verifier can query. Swapping the commitment scheme changes a system's proof size, its trust assumptions, and its post-quantum status while leaving the <fr:link href="/dhsorens-002R/" title="Arithmetisation" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-002R/" display-uri="dhsorens-002R" type="local">arithmetisation</fr:link> untouched — which is why this is the layer where systems are usually differentiated.
</html:p>
            <html:p><fr:link href="/dhsorens-0040/" title="ArkLib" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-0040/" display-uri="dhsorens-0040" type="local">ArkLib</fr:link> splits these into ordinary and functional commitments; understanding why that split is the right one is part of the point of these pages.
</html:p>
