              <fr:mainmatter>
                <html:p>
    The commitment underneath every hash-based system. Simple enough that it is easy to skip and consequential enough that the <fr:link href="/dhsorens-0048/" title="ZK-Friendly Primitives" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-0048/" display-uri="dhsorens-0048" type="local">hash choice</fr:link> dominates prover time.
</html:p>
                <html:p>
    Questions this page should answer:
    <html:ul><html:li>Position binding, and what a Merkle commitment does not give you.</html:li>
        <html:li>Batched openings and path deduplication; where proof size actually goes.</html:li>
        <html:li>Salting and its role in <fr:link href="/dhsorens-002E/" title="Interactive Proofs, Arguments, and IOPs" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-002E/" display-uri="dhsorens-002E" type="local">zero knowledge</fr:link> — and in ArkLib's <html:code>Salt.lean</html:code>.</html:li>
        <html:li>Hash cost as the prover's real bottleneck; arity and tree shape tradeoffs.</html:li>
        <html:li>The formalisation: what ArkLib's Merkle tree development proves, and what it assumes about the hash.</html:li></html:ul></html:p>
