              <fr:mainmatter>
                <html:p>
    The tensor-commitment lineage: commit to a matrix whose rows are codewords, and test proximity by sampling columns. Basefold is where this line and the <fr:link href="/dhsorens-0037/" title="FRI" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-0037/" display-uri="dhsorens-0037" type="local">FRI</fr:link> line merge.
</html:p>
                <html:p>
    Questions this page should answer:
    <html:ul><html:li>The tensor structure and the proximity test; why the verifier's work is <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[O(\sqrt {n})]]></fr:tex> in the plain construction.</html:li>
        <html:li>Brakedown's linear-time encodable codes, and the tradeoff against proof size.</html:li>
        <html:li>Basefold: foldable codes plus <fr:link href="/dhsorens-002Q/" title="The Sumcheck Protocol" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-002Q/" display-uri="dhsorens-002Q" type="local">sumcheck</fr:link>, giving a multilinear commitment from a code that need not be Reed–Solomon.</html:li>
        <html:li>Which codes are admissible, and what the soundness proof needs from them.</html:li>
        <html:li>How this family compares with <fr:link href="/dhsorens-003C/" title="WHIR" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-003C/" display-uri="dhsorens-003C" type="local">WHIR</fr:link> on verifier cost and on argument size.</html:li></html:ul></html:p>
                <html:p>
    Sources: <fr:link href="https://eprint.iacr.org/2026/1367" type="external">SoK: Hash-Based Polynomial Commitments and Low-Degree Tests</fr:link>.
