          <fr:mainmatter>
            <html:p>
    The tower <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathbb {F}_2 \subset  \mathbb {F}_{2^2} \subset  \mathbb {F}_{2^4} \subset  \cdots  \subset  \mathbb {F}_{2^{128}}]]></fr:tex>, built by iterated quadratic extension, and the arithmetic that makes it attractive. This is the ground <fr:link href="/dhsorens-003J/" title="Binius" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-003J/" display-uri="dhsorens-003J" type="local">Binius</fr:link> stands on.
</html:p>
            <html:p>
    Questions this page should answer:
    <html:ul><html:li>The tower construction, and why every subfield embedding is free.</html:li>
        <html:li>Why binary: no modular reduction, characteristic 2 arithmetic maps onto bit operations, and small values cost small fields.</html:li>
        <html:li>The claimed cost comparison against Mersenne31, and under what measure it holds.</html:li>
        <html:li>Multilinear polynomials over a tower, and why the multilinear setting rather than the univariate one.</html:li>
        <html:li><fr:link href="/dhsorens-0035/" title="Small-Field Commitments and Ring Switching" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-0035/" display-uri="dhsorens-0035" type="local">Ring switching</fr:link>: moving between tower levels inside a single argument.</html:li></html:ul></html:p>
            <html:p>
    Sources: <fr:link href="https://www.irreducible.com/posts/binary-tower-fields-are-the-future-of-verifiable-computing" type="external">Binary tower fields</fr:link> (Irreducible), <fr:link href="https://blog.lambdaclass.com/snarks-on-binary-fields-binius/" type="external">SNARKs on binary fields</fr:link> (LambdaClass), <fr:link href="https://eprint.iacr.org/2026/1371" type="external">The Small-Field Turn</fr:link>.
</html:p>
